<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.6.2: Using Diagrams to Understand Equivalent Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02178</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.6.2: Using Diagrams to Understand Equivalent Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7424a99c-0b01-423f-b6a1-b8a971f18997</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
        <para><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_8_6_2_PLA_72"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
       
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
        <para id="para-00038"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_8_6_2_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
       
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Using Diagrams to Understand Equivalent Expressions</title>
        <para id="para-00048">
          <emphasis>Example 1</emphasis>
       </para>
        <para id="para-00049">Look at these two expressions.</para>
        <para id="para-00050"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00051"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00052">We can show they are equivalent by using a diagram to break apart the different pieces.</para>
        <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - First, let’s populate the diagram.</para>
        <para id="para-00054">
              <media alt="DIAGRAM THAT SHOWS MULTIPLICATION OF X PLUS 5 TIMES X PLUS 3. X AND 5 ARE ON THE LEFT AND X AND 3 ARE ON THE TOP. THERE ARE 4 SECTIONS TO BE FILLED IN.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_08_05_01.jpg"/>
            </media>
          
       </para>
        <para id="para-00055"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Then we multiply the corresponding rows and columns.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-doubleheadertable os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00009">
          <tgroup cols="3">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry> </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> 3 </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> 5 </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00056">This leaves the diagram shown here.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-doubleheadertable os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00010">
          <tgroup cols="3">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry> </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> 3 </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> 5 </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                <entry> 15 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - We can use this diagram to find our expression in standard form.</para>
        <para id="para-00058">
              <media alt="DIAGRAM THAT REPRESENTS MULTIPLICATION OF X PLUS 5 TIMES X PLUS 3. X AND 5 ARE ON THE LEFT AND X AND 3 ARE ON THE TOP. THE 4 SECTIONS ARE FILLED IN AS X-SQUARED, 3X, 5X, AND 15. THE PRODUCT OF X-SQUARED PLUS 8X PLUS 15 IS SHOWN, WITH A GREEN ARROW FROM X-SQUARED IN THE DIAGRAM TO X-SQUARED IN THE EXPRESSION, TWO BLUE ARROWS FROM 3X AND 5X IN THE DIAGRAM TO 8X IN THE EXPRESSION, AND A RED ARROW FROM 15 IN THE DIAGRAM TO 15 IN THE EXPRESSION.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_08_05_02.jpg"/>
            </media>
          
       </para>
        <para id="para-00059">So, the expressions are equivalent.</para>
        <para id="para-00060">
          <emphasis>Example 2</emphasis>
       </para>
        <para id="para-00061">The same can be done with two factors using subtraction as well.</para>
        <para id="para-00062"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00063"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>54</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00064">It is helpful to rewrite the factored form as the addition of negative numbers when subtracting occurs in the factors.</para>
        <para id="para-00065">So <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> becomes <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Now the process is the same.</para>
        <para id="para-00066"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Let’s populate the table.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-doubleheadertable os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00011">
          <tgroup cols="3">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry> </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> –6 </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> –9 </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
                <entry> </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00067"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the columns and rows.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-doubleheadertable os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00012">
          <tgroup cols="3">
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <entry> </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> –6 </emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis> –9 </emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                <entry> 54 </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <para id="para-00068"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Use the diagram to write the expression in standard form.</para>
        <para id="para-00069"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>54</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00070">The expressions are equivalent.</para>
        <para id="para-00071">When the coefficients of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-terms are both 1 in the factored form:</para>
        <para id="para-00072">Take notice that the linear term (<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-term) in the expression in standard form is the sum of the two numbers in the expression in factored form.</para>
        <list id="list-00001">
          <item>In the example above, the term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is the sum of the two numbers –9 and –6.</item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00073">Also, the constant term is the product of the two numbers in the expression in factored form.</para>
        <list id="list-00002">
          <item>In the example above, the term 54 is the product of the two numbers –9 and –6.</item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00074">Recognizing this will be helpful for switching between standard and factored forms. Remember, this pattern only occurs when the coefficients of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-terms in the factors are 1 (because they result in a quadratic term that has a coefficient of 1 as well, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>).</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Using Diagrams to Understand Equivalent Expressions</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
            <problem id="prob-00008">
              <para id="para-00075">Use a diagram to write <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> in standard form.</para>
              <table class="unnumbered os-raise-doubleheadertable os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00013">
                <tgroup cols="3">
                  <tbody>
                    <row>
                      <entry> </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> –3 </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> –8 </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                      <entry> 24 </entry>
                    </row>
                  </tbody>
                </tgroup>
              </table>
              <para id="para-00076">Based on the diagram, what is an equivalent factored form of this expression?</para>
              <para id="para-00077">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00008">
              <para id="para-00078">Here is how to use a diagram to help convert between standard and factored forms:</para>
              <para id="para-00079"><m:math><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><table class="unnumbered os-raise-doubleheadertable os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00014">
                <tgroup cols="3">
                  <tbody>
                    <row>
                      <entry> </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> 2 </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> 7 </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                      <entry> 14 </entry>
                    </row>
                  </tbody>
                </tgroup>
              </table>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
            <problem id="prob-00009">
              <para id="para-00080">The diagram shown breaks down <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>11</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>24</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
              <table class="unnumbered os-raise-doubleheadertable os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00015">
                <tgroup cols="3">
                  <tbody>
                    <row>
                      <entry> </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> –3 </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                      <entry>
                        <emphasis> –8 </emphasis>
                      </entry>
                      <entry> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> </entry>
                      <entry> 24 </entry>
                    </row>
                  </tbody>
                </tgroup>
              </table>
              <para id="para-00081">Based on the diagram, what is an equivalent factored form of this expression?</para>
              <para id="para-00082">Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00009">
              <para id="para-00083">Here is how to use a diagram to help convert between standard and factored forms:</para>
              <para id="para-00084"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>